(3) Keyboard reach / accessible name / caption: PASS. Focus ring: FAIL in
dark mode.
  - Tab reachability: real `page.keyboard.press('Tab')` loop from body
    reached `div[role=region][aria-label="Paired and external transfers"]`
    in 4 (390/light), 2 (390/dark), 11 (1280/light), 1 (1280/dark) presses;
    `activeElement` matched role=region and aria-label exactly in all four.
  - sr-only caption: `<caption class="sr-only">Paired and external
    transfers</caption>` (line 128) is unchanged and still present — the
    region's aria-label duplicates it, which the contract explicitly
    permits ("fine — note it"). OBSERVATION only.
  - Focus ring visibility: measured by screenshotting the region's
    bounding box (+20px pad) before and after a REAL keyboard Tab reached
    it (not a synthetic `.focus()` call — that contaminates Chromium's
    `:focus-visible` input-modality heuristic after a prior mouse click on
    the theme toggle and gives a false negative; verified via
    `el.matches(':focus-visible')` = true in both themes for the keyboard
    path used here), then diffing pixels with Pillow.
    Light (1280): 2016 px differ; outline color measured `rgb(16,16,16)`
    against a `rgb(255,255,255)` page background → contrast ≈ 19:1. Clearly
    visible (confirmed visually in the screenshot: a dark ring appears
    around the region).
    Dark (1280): 2416 px differ (ring IS painted — this is real, not the
    zero-diff artifact from the contaminated-modality test I discarded),
    but the outline color measured is the SAME literal `rgb(16,16,16)`
    (Chromium's `outline: auto` resolves to a fixed near-black, not a true
    per-pixel invert, in this headless build) against the region's dark
    background/border pixels (`rgb(41,37,36)` / `rgb(68,64,60)`, the
    dark-theme stone-800/gray-700 tones already in use on this page).
    Contrast ratio computed (WCAG relative-luminance formula):
      rgb(16,16,16) vs rgb(41,37,36)  → ≈ 1.25:1
      rgb(16,16,16) vs rgb(68,64,60)  → ≈ 1.85:1
    Both far below the 3:1 floor for UI-component/state boundaries. The
    ring is technically painted but is not a visible focus indicator to a
    normal-vision user in dark mode — this is exactly the "control visible
    in one theme, invisible in the other" case the audit brief calls out
    as a FAIL, not an OBSERVATION.

FAILURES:
1. Constitution point 9 ("focus indicator is visible") + point 7 (UI
   component boundaries ≥3:1) + point 12 ("dark mode has full parity: all
   contrast requirements (7) hold in both themes") + WCAG 2.2 AA 1.4.11
   Non-Text Contrast (focus indicators are UI-component state markers
   under 1.4.11's own Understanding doc).
   File: web/templates/pages/transfers.html:126 (the new
   `tabindex="0" role="region"` div).
   Observed: the region relies on the browser's unstyled default
   `outline: auto` focus ring. In light mode this renders at ~19:1
   contrast (fine); in dark mode the SAME literal outline color renders at
   ~1.25–1.85:1 against the region's dark-theme background/border — not a
   visible focus indicator. axe-core does not catch this because its
   color-contrast rule targets text, not non-text UI-state contrast, which
   is exactly the manual-check gap this audit is required to walk.
   Attribution: the failing markup itself (`tabindex="0" role="region"` on
   this div) is new content introduced by BL3, not byte-identical to
   master — `git diff master` above shows it is the only change in this
   file. The unstyled-outline PATTERN it uses is a pre-existing convention
   (master already has an identical `tabindex="0" role="region"` wrapper
   with no custom focus style at web/templates/pages/major-expenses.html:667,
   confirmed via `git show master:web/templates/pages/major-expenses.html
   | grep -n tabindex`), so this is a known defect class being replicated
   into new content, not one BL3 invented from scratch — per the run's own
   attribution rule this still FAILs the task (only byte-identical
   unchanged markup is an OBSERVATION; replication into new content is
   not), but the fix belongs to the same styles.css convention the project
   already uses elsewhere for exactly this reason
   (`[data-wf-tab]:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid rgb(var(--accent));
   outline-offset: 2px; }`, styles.css:231-238) — add the new region
   selector to that block (or an equivalent) rather than leaving it on the
   native default.

